#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064003 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "48ec6930-88b7-5aad-9bb7-c5e23e766f80" :question/math-academy-id "e-470" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $9x^2 = (3x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\,\\textrm d u=\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x)^{2}}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/id #uuid "3e89a278-54ab-59a6-adb6-38f43c0fbe9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-471" :question/problem "Calculate the integral  $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{3}{2(1 + 25x^2)} = \\dfrac 3 2 \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+25x^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^2}\\textrm{d}u =\\arctan u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $25x^2 = (5x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 5\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2+50{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡5x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "cb771ff3-b5a9-5900-913f-df23957760b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-472" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt 2} \\dfrac{1}{2+x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+{x}^{2}/2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this integral now resembles the basic integral for the inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{1+u^2}\\,\\textrm d u = \\arctan{u} + C.\n$$\n\nWe make the substitution $u=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt 2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sqrt 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064014 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/id #uuid "56860611-869d-5035-bf8f-f568c774d78d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5014" :question/problem "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5\\, \\textrm{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^2-1}}  = }$" :question/worked-solution "This resembles the basic integral for $\\textrm{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\,\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}} & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06683b40-3991-5149-8a0a-86a938d43b3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296135} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3bf19e-e0ab-5cb9-90b1-f43021992e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296136} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0c7cee-5881-5103-bd91-3df14f29b289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296137} {:answer/id #uuid "98a5a131-b799-5b3b-a0a3-c57bf567eeab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296138} {:answer/id #uuid "de7379ac-edf1-5569-8713-27d0beec1603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296139}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98a5a131-b799-5b3b-a0a3-c57bf567eeab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296138} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d1f923a-4ff1-53c6-af71-81359ee48ab3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fa09994-b03e-519d-aa09-462602c3715d" :question/math-academy-id "q-114032" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $3{x}^{2}=(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{3}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-3{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161035dd-534e-5c12-88df-5fe26ee6a1e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296142} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f91561-2994-50d4-8899-964b34007fb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296143} {:answer/id #uuid "6870e8fd-8f88-5aed-8b88-d157b85205d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296144} {:answer/id #uuid "79dd410d-7feb-59d2-8793-065ac1904021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296145} {:answer/id #uuid "966fc9a3-c000-5205-81d7-a858502c46c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296146}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79dd410d-7feb-59d2-8793-065ac1904021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296145} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b5e482-b9e5-5c06-9080-0da05e7a456c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "229d26fc-7842-5b85-86ac-4396d46bdbad" :question/math-academy-id "q-114045" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{8}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{8}{16\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/16)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/16}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{4}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41586ce1-c84e-52f5-afa7-4a8d0c32f0e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296149} {:answer/id #uuid "d8377633-bacf-5c7c-b510-899fe804aff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(3x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296150} {:answer/id #uuid "74c30c11-17d0-5fed-aef3-6138bd144eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\text{arcsec}(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296151} {:answer/id #uuid "ea14f68c-bd33-5965-bba7-bb3e19cfd9e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{arcsec}(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296152} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd4ad11-4f50-547b-a2a7-ac496d7c777a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dd4ad11-4f50-547b-a2a7-ac496d7c777a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{3}x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296153} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cc0e267-f80c-5ee2-a278-454fed19ebbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bc2c1d59-0a7c-5002-a982-86e3a36461a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-114053" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|x|\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}-1}}=\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{|\\sqrt{3}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $\\sqrt{3}x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\sqrt{3}x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{|\\sqrt{3}x|\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3}x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(\\sqrt{3}x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99be1b86-835e-5661-b812-7c51eada2625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(50x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365160} {:answer/id #uuid "54973dfd-f90c-5026-8d70-0a961f0fa969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{arcsec}(10x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365161} {:answer/id #uuid "19f71a9c-1f87-5075-b21b-2d4b2e21592c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(10x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365162} {:answer/id #uuid "7fcdb187-2b0b-5f4b-b40f-c9c86d70fd50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(10x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365163} {:answer/id #uuid "cec3cb53-2ae0-5e60-90ca-1d3624a6b211" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19f71a9c-1f87-5075-b21b-2d4b2e21592c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{arcsec}(10x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365162} :answer-field/id #uuid "5597e1dc-82cd-543b-80ca-58e11a0d672b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "28b96c5a-8c44-50c1-abfb-4319bd1a0d5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114057" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{100{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{100{x}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{50}{|10x|\\sqrt{(10x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $10x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=10x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{10}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{100{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{50}{|10x|\\sqrt{(10x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =50\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =50\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{arcsec}(10x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6899a725-4d66-5761-b512-2fe14b6449c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337697} {:answer/id #uuid "6f16e828-9dcf-58a5-b632-4ae0241bcc01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6899a725-4d66-5761-b512-2fe14b6449c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337697} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2a6b1b6-12a6-50d5-8c5d-8ceb4cdd7556" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5586c350-c98f-5ffa-862c-03fe553a31e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-351834" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-75{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-75{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3\\cdot (1-25{x}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-25{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(5x)}^{2}.$   \n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $-0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3-75{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{-0.2}^{0.2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(5x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5d97224-db0b-5626-bc42-c9a729cbe65e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328969} {:answer/id #uuid "61fa916e-ddd5-5d7e-9a54-81a3de5873cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5d97224-db0b-5626-bc42-c9a729cbe65e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328969} :answer-field/id #uuid "f84bc2aa-3f4d-5d08-8ffe-b72105efb2cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "264f6e72-6e53-5a6d-a4d9-04f39e481ab6" :question/math-academy-id "q-351855" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{12-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{12-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{12\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/12)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{12}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/12}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{12}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/\\sqrt{12})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{12}})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{12}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{12}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{12}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{12-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{12}}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/\\sqrt{12})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{12}}{\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{12}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebe8b81e-9d29-5285-b27c-39067fdda4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\arctan (3x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063251} {:answer/id #uuid "f822bacb-c50f-5046-b9ef-7e15ffe9f297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\arctan (3x))/(3) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063252} {:answer/id #uuid "186632c2-fc0f-5219-a5b0-d739ed584564" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\arcsin (3x))/(3) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063253} {:answer/id #uuid "38701fa4-6057-5699-a3c6-c3a19a2e3f38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\arcsin (3x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063254} {:answer/id #uuid "5674be11-46d0-5e8f-bf62-814fc82a205e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\arctan (3x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebe8b81e-9d29-5285-b27c-39067fdda4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\arctan (3x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063251} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1916893-3a98-5964-91fa-00bf5c3bddad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ff7c3e1-6732-5fb3-bcd2-7ed1aeab0712" :question/math-academy-id "q-49540" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{6}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $9{x}^{2}=(3x)^{2}.$\n\nSo. let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int \\frac{1}{1+(3x)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064791 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eae4fd41-8d6f-56b8-927f-c9985266451f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\arctan (\\frac{x}{7}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063257} {:answer/id #uuid "1d15a464-b583-5fc8-b316-ebe108dcd317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\arcsin (\\frac{x}{7}))/(7) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063258} {:answer/id #uuid "2ac1f393-2938-5440-b9aa-dfd8864a8793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\arctan (7x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063259} {:answer/id #uuid "5df51dba-e91d-5dcd-9b71-fcec9dc63f3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\arcsin (\\frac{x}{7}))/(3) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063260} {:answer/id #uuid "7d44e4b9-9e22-5ff7-a617-af544b2f2d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\arcsin (\\frac{x}{7}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d44e4b9-9e22-5ff7-a617-af544b2f2d17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\arcsin (\\frac{x}{7}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063261} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cfb6d85-1b96-50ff-b071-589877187c99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20c23af2-eaef-5502-8cf2-7e2560ab8597" :question/math-academy-id "q-49547" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{49-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{49-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{49\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/49)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/49}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/7)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{7})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{7}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{7}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=7\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{49-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/7)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (7\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{7})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c69be993-3132-5696-9b79-e5b3d281cbd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{9}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063263} {:answer/id #uuid "cfcaa2b3-e4ea-55b2-b6dc-e88a4ed4c2f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\arctan (\\frac{x}{9}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063264} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0d0bff-f768-510e-b5c4-691f98a3f859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{9}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063265} {:answer/id #uuid "1d5d6d23-685a-5bcc-9d0a-4b5e34a7b9c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\arctan (\\frac{x}{3}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063266} {:answer/id #uuid "3859c10f-d795-5c84-b6f5-969568b4922a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{3}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3859c10f-d795-5c84-b6f5-969568b4922a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\arctan (\\frac{x}{3}) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063267} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff188bde-6bcb-5810-aef6-1e39a9361870" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea76ea1f-1600-5c9d-b198-b9fb197580ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-49548" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{9\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/9)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44e7dabb-eac3-5be4-a822-9e83ed502365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328980} {:answer/id #uuid "5ced324b-5e0c-5279-8fcb-1672a0195c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328981} {:answer/id #uuid "0d5e154d-38e9-56ca-b0a6-e00f00525f23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(6x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328982} {:answer/id #uuid "b413e93d-3ea1-5565-ab88-37ce7a34aff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328983} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb38827-0f8c-5f6c-a724-802ecadb3b55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ced324b-5e0c-5279-8fcb-1672a0195c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328981} :answer-field/id #uuid "10ae71b0-b6c2-5526-8818-fc4f96a69613" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e1b8c51-6836-5b3c-99de-c30519910bed" :question/math-academy-id "q-73714" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{36\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/36)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/36}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{6}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{6}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=6\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/6)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (6\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{6})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328985 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15740c5e-8d47-509f-88bf-a5b76d5705f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(6x)}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328987} {:answer/id #uuid "defbd277-ef1e-505b-b285-7b9186d0ab3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(6x)}{36}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328988} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c18280-84aa-55da-bbed-ddb7c6554654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{arcsec}(36x)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328989} {:answer/id #uuid "060d0dc4-dd76-56cb-a1d4-a7bd504be2e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(36x)}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328990} {:answer/id #uuid "2a00391a-1173-5adc-939e-b9d2cfc6c19e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(6x)}{6}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15740c5e-8d47-509f-88bf-a5b76d5705f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(6x)}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328987} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f6d69e8-0145-519c-8f00-67004d2af730" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6a84188-2fa3-529a-a3ac-082b44a3cc5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-73715" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{|6x|\\sqrt{(6x)^{2}-1}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{|6x|\\sqrt{(6x)^{2}-1}}=3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{|6x|\\sqrt{(6x)^{2}-1}}.\n$$\n\nThis integral now resembles the basic one for $\\text{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $6x.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=6x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3\\int \\frac{1}{|6x|\\sqrt{(6x)^{2}-1}}\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{arcsec }u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{arcsec}(6x)}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88f90975-0515-58b0-a084-10ecd3313242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4arcsec(2x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063682} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1ae62a-5d7c-553f-b91b-e9122ab8f92c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "arcsec(2x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063683} {:answer/id #uuid "061e358a-a593-5647-a79f-4c6fdcd9b43e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(arcsec(2x))/(2) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063684} {:answer/id #uuid "2d743270-6050-5a4c-b34e-816cb83e91dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2arcsec(2x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063685} {:answer/id #uuid "371decde-a130-5186-8557-fccc9516569a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(arcsec(2x))/(4) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d743270-6050-5a4c-b34e-816cb83e91dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2arcsec(2x) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063685} :answer-field/id #uuid "694f5765-dba3-5307-89dc-9abeef044716" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063681}] :question/id #uuid "a6fafd98-c438-5a64-a87a-7a2d0a58fd7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73717" :question/problem "What is $∫\\frac{2}{\\mid x \\mid \\sqrt{4x^{2} - 1}}dx$?"}]}
